      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The development of Park Hills started in the 1920s, decades after cities like Erlanger, Elsmere, and Crescent Springs were founded.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.phcahub.org/the-history-of-park-hills/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the 1800s, Park Hills was mostly uninhabited—too hilly to farm and too inaccessible for homes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.phcahub.org/the-history-of-park-hills/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The only landmark in the area was the Stonewall House, a restaurant tavern built sometime in the mid‑1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.phcahub.org/the-history-of-park-hills/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Trolley Park was named in honor of the trolley line that ran through it.",
